  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "From **left to right**, the ellipse $25{x}^{2}+14{y}^{2}-50x-11y=29$ intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}\n$)$",
  "worked_solution": "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}+14{y}^{2}-50x-11y & =29 \\\\ 25{x}^{2}+14(0)^{2}-50x-11(0) & =29 \\\\ 25{x}^{2}-50x & =29 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =\\frac{29}{25} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2} & =\\frac{29}{25} \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{29}{25}+1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{29}{25}+\\frac{25}{25} \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{54}{25} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{5} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(1-\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{5},0)\\,\\text{and}\\,B(1+\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{5},0).\n$$",
